1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:05,484
Time to run the whole section on something real, not a worked example from Harlan's

2
00:00:05,484 --> 00:00:05,978
account.

3
00:00:05,978 --> 00:00:10,988
Pull up one ad set from the testing architecture you built in Section 7.5, or the

4
00:00:10,988 --> 00:00:13,895
closest thing you have to live spend right now.

5
00:00:13,895 --> 00:00:17,359
Work through it in the same order the section taught it.

6
00:00:17,359 --> 00:00:22,554
First, name the objective and the metric that actually answers it, from Section 8.1.

7
00:00:22,554 --> 00:00:27,688
Then run Section 8.2's three questions in order: kill rule margin, CAC ceiling gap,

8
00:00:27,688 --> 00:00:28,986
swap-test robustness.

9
00:00:28,986 --> 00:00:34,120
Only once that produces an actual verdict do you move on to fatigue and the iterate

10
00:00:34,120 --> 00:00:36,656
call, and only if the verdict was winner.

11
00:00:36,656 --> 00:00:39,068
Resist the urge to skip to the verdict.

12
00:00:39,068 --> 00:00:44,140
The whole point of scoring it step by step is that a wrong answer at step one, the

13
00:00:44,140 --> 00:00:49,273
wrong metric for the objective, quietly produces a wrong verdict at step three, and

14
00:00:49,273 --> 00:00:53,355
you would never see the mistake if you jumped straight to the end.

15
00:00:53,355 --> 00:00:58,551
Pick the ad set you're least certain about first, not the one you already suspect is

16
00:00:58,551 --> 00:00:59,046
winning.

17
00:00:59,046 --> 00:01:00,035
Why start there?

18
00:01:00,035 --> 00:01:05,169
The scorecard earns its keep on exactly that kind of ad set, the one where your gut

19
00:01:05,169 --> 00:01:07,148
hasn't already made up its mind.

20
00:01:07,148 --> 00:01:10,611
Scoring an obvious winner mostly confirms what you knew.

21
00:01:10,611 --> 00:01:14,508
Scoring an ambiguous one is where this section pays for itself.

22
00:01:15,058 --> 00:01:20,032
Seven lines, and notice the structure mirrors the section itself: the first four

23
00:01:20,032 --> 00:01:24,944
lines are Section 8.2's tree, the last three only apply once that tree actually

24
00:01:24,944 --> 00:01:26,064
produces a winner.

25
00:01:26,064 --> 00:01:28,924
A loser or a noise verdict stops at line four.

26
00:01:28,924 --> 00:01:34,084
There's nothing to check for fatigue on an angle that never won in the first place.

27
00:01:34,084 --> 00:01:37,007
Fill in line one before you look at any number.

28
00:01:37,007 --> 00:01:41,981
Decide what you were actually testing, then find the metric that answers it, the

29
00:01:41,981 --> 00:01:44,095
same discipline Section 8.1 built.

30
00:01:44,095 --> 00:01:49,132
Writing the objective down first, before the numbers are in front of you, is what

31
00:01:49,132 --> 00:01:50,562
keeps line four honest.

32
00:01:50,562 --> 00:01:55,474
Lines five through seven only get filled in once, per ad set, at whatever point

33
00:01:55,474 --> 00:01:57,153
you're actually reading it.

34
00:01:57,153 --> 00:02:01,754
Don't pre-fill a fatigue guess before you check the real frequency number.

35
00:02:01,754 --> 00:02:06,790
That's the same mistake Section 8.3 already warned against: reacting to a feeling

36
00:02:06,790 --> 00:02:10,894
instead of the column that's sitting right there on the dashboard.

37
00:02:10,894 --> 00:02:15,868
Keep one filled-in scorecard per ad set, dated, rather than overwriting the same

38
00:02:15,868 --> 00:02:17,733
sheet every time you check in.

39
00:02:17,733 --> 00:02:21,588
A single snapshot only tells you where an ad set stands today.

40
00:02:21,588 --> 00:02:26,563
A dated series of them is what actually lets you read the pattern across refresh

41
00:02:26,563 --> 00:02:31,599
cycles that Section 8.4 asked you to watch for, weeks apart, not just this week's

42
00:02:31,599 --> 00:02:32,843
number in isolation.

43
00:02:33,393 --> 00:02:38,123
About fifteen minutes per ad set, most of it in the tree and the final decision

44
00:02:38,123 --> 00:02:43,154
rather than the fatigue check, which is a single number once you know where to look.

45
00:02:43,154 --> 00:02:48,004
Run this on every ad set currently live, not just the one you suspect is winning.

46
00:02:48,004 --> 00:02:52,675
A quiet loser sitting unflagged in your account costs you exactly as much as a

47
00:02:52,675 --> 00:02:54,831
genuine winner you failed to notice.

48
00:02:54,831 --> 00:02:59,502
If this is your first time running the tree on real numbers, expect it to feel

49
00:02:59,502 --> 00:03:02,256
slower than reading a dashboard normally does.

50
00:03:02,256 --> 00:03:04,352
That's the scorecard doing its job.

51
00:03:04,352 --> 00:03:09,023
A dashboard read in ten seconds skips straight to the verdict your gut already

52
00:03:09,023 --> 00:03:09,442
wanted.

53
00:03:09,442 --> 00:03:14,473
Fifteen minutes per ad set is what it costs to check that verdict against the actual

54
00:03:14,473 --> 00:03:15,251
data instead.

55
00:03:15,251 --> 00:03:19,982
It gets faster the second and third time through, once the three questions stop

56
00:03:19,982 --> 00:03:24,832
needing conscious effort and start feeling like the obvious way to read a result.

57
00:03:24,832 --> 00:03:28,186
That's the actual goal here, not speed for its own sake.

58
00:03:28,186 --> 00:03:32,977
A scorecard you have to think hard about every single time is one you'll quietly

59
00:03:32,977 --> 00:03:35,611
start skipping under real deadline pressure.

60
00:03:35,611 --> 00:03:38,426
One that's become a habit survives a busy week.

61
00:03:38,976 --> 00:03:42,177
One honest expectation before you call this finished.

62
00:03:42,177 --> 00:03:47,250
If you score five ad sets and only one earns a clean winner verdict, check your work

63
00:03:47,250 --> 00:03:51,417
against Section 8.2's tree again before you assume something's wrong.

64
00:03:51,417 --> 00:03:56,189
That ratio is what an honest batch actually looks like, not a sign your testing

65
00:03:56,189 --> 00:03:57,638
architecture is failing.

66
00:03:57,638 --> 00:04:02,591
The scorecard is only doing its job if it sometimes tells you something you didn't

67
00:04:02,591 --> 00:04:07,362
want to hear: an ad set you liked lands on loser, or one you'd already mentally

68
00:04:07,362 --> 00:04:10,563
written off turns out to have cleared every question.

69
00:04:10,563 --> 00:04:12,918
Either result is the scorecard working.

70
00:04:12,918 --> 00:04:17,569
A scorecard that only ever confirms what you already believed isn't measuring

71
00:04:17,569 --> 00:04:21,071
anything, it's just restating your taste with extra steps.

72
00:04:21,071 --> 00:04:25,480
Notice, too, that a noise verdict is not a failure to reach a conclusion.

73
00:04:25,480 --> 00:04:30,372
It is the conclusion, exactly as real as winner or loser, and Section 8.2 already

74
00:04:30,372 --> 00:04:35,144
asked you to write it down plainly rather than treat it as an unfinished result

75
00:04:35,144 --> 00:04:36,895
waiting for a clearer answer.

76
00:04:36,895 --> 00:04:38,948
Some ad sets stay noise for weeks.

77
00:04:38,948 --> 00:04:43,599
Recording that honestly, week after week, is still more useful than forcing a

78
00:04:43,599 --> 00:04:46,015
verdict the data hasn't actually earned.

79
00:04:46,565 --> 00:04:48,517
One lecture left in this section.

80
00:04:48,517 --> 00:04:53,427
Then Section nine takes the scoring habit you just built and turns it into a weekly

81
00:04:53,427 --> 00:04:57,331
rhythm, so it happens every week without needing its own reminder.

82
00:04:57,331 --> 00:05:02,182
Before that, finish scoring whatever's currently live in your account, even the ad

83
00:05:02,182 --> 00:05:04,253
sets you're fairly sure are losers.

84
00:05:04,253 --> 00:05:05,259
Especially those.

85
00:05:05,259 --> 00:05:09,873
A scorecard filled in only for the ad sets you expect to win isn't honest, and

86
00:05:09,873 --> 00:05:13,481
honest is the entire point of everything this section taught.

87
00:05:13,481 --> 00:05:18,332
Ask yourself one last question before you close this out: would the version of you

88
00:05:18,332 --> 00:05:21,586
from before this section have caught the same verdicts?

89
00:05:21,586 --> 00:05:26,082
If the honest answer is no, on even one ad set, that's the actual value this

90
00:05:26,082 --> 00:05:30,164
workshop delivered, not a nice-to-have alongside the artifact itself.
